(rule
 ; ocannl-backend: none -- runs the benchmark orchestrator's own Python unit tests, which import
 ; orchestrate.py and bench_common.py and launch no OCANNL executable.
 (alias runtest-test_orchestrate)
 ; The rule runs sandboxed, so the test sees exactly the files listed below and nothing a
 ; neighbouring rule happened to copy into _build/default/benchmarks: an unlisted file is absent on
 ; every run, not only on a fresh build (the gh-ocannl-586/597 hazard, in Python -- unsandboxed,
 ; this directory's own ocannl_config went unlisted and passed wherever another rule had copied
 ; it). `preserve_file_kind` rules out the symlink sandbox, which would not isolate anything here:
 ; Python puts a symlinked script's RESOLVED directory on sys.path, and the test locates its data
 ; through `Path(__file__).resolve()`, so both would land back in _build/default/benchmarks. Dune
 ; plants an empty `.git` above every sandbox, so no test may read the enclosing checkout's
 ; repository; the source-identity test builds a repository of its own.
 ;
 ; The Python modules are globbed rather than named: the test grows imports faster than anyone
 ; remembers to pin them. Today it imports orchestrate, strict_json, bench_output, display_safe,
 ; gh675_cells, gh998_cells, gh1002_cells, gen_fixtures, bench_venv, cell_group, fixture_digest,
 ; runners.bench_common, test.test_cell_group and test_cells, runs gh1003_summary.py,
 ; gh612_parity.py, gh728_protocol.py and ab.py by path, and its venv-interpreter scan reads every
 ; `*.py` here and under runners/ at any depth -- hence the recursive glob, the same population as
 ; the scan's `runners/**/*.py`. The shell scripts are listed for the same reason: the test drives
 ; gh612_cells.sh's parity step, gh1003_fold_cells.sh's summary step, and gh1343_ab.sh and
 ; gh1180_tie_ab.sh end to end on stub benches (test_cells.py) themselves, and its
 ; json.loads inventory and its table-row scan read every `*.py` and `*.sh` here and under runners/
 ; (an unlisted script is one they silently cannot see).
 ; fixtures/DIGESTS.txt and the workload specs are data it reads (gh-ocannl-645), pinned so a
 ; change to them fails the suite rather than drifting -- and so is the golden of
 ; test/operations/bench_result_line, which holds OCANNL result lines this orchestrator's own
 ; parser has to accept (gh-ocannl-676). ocannl_config.reference is where the orchestrator reads
 ; the configuration keys a per-cell flag may name and the keys each profile's payload sets
 ; (gh-ocannl-1273); this directory's own ocannl_config is what the test checks sets no key that
 ; would stand between a cell and its cache.
 (deps
  ocannl_config
  (glob_files *.py)
  (glob_files test/*.py)
  (glob_files_rec runners/*.py)
  (glob_files *.sh)
  (glob_files_rec runners/*.sh)
  fixtures/DIGESTS.txt
  ../ocannl_config.reference
  ../test/operations/bench_result_line.expected
  (glob_files workloads/*.json)
  (sandbox always preserve_file_kind))
 (action
  (run python3 %{dep:test_orchestrate.py})))

; This directory's golden-diff rules, aggregated back onto `runtest` (gh-ocannl-726). Each of them
; attaches to its own `runtest-<name>` alias and to NOTHING else, so that `dune build
; @benchmarks/runtest-test_orchestrate` runs that one test and applies its diff; this stanza is what
; keeps `dune runtest` running all of them. Attaching such a rule to `runtest` as well would defeat
; the point: a rule on two aliases makes building either alias build both, so a per-test alias that
; also named `runtest` would pull the whole directory in behind it -- and where the name is one dune
; generates for a `(test)` stanza, dune rejects the pair outright as a dependency cycle. Hence the
; naming: `<name>` is the golden the rule checks, and never a `(test)` stanza's name in this
; directory. A `runtest-<name>` rule missing from this list is one plain `dune runtest` skips
; silently, which `test/operations/env_var_deps` checks -- the same check it makes of the `slow`
; suite.

(alias
 (name runtest)
 (deps
  (alias runtest-test_orchestrate)))
